  "problem": "If $A$ is the maximum value of $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ then $A=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $2$ units **up**.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255475/assets/20c3c4c86b54f69f547c9ad64f99f2ef4aaa506a5466f355839fbe0b7761e98d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $2$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\le 1+2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2$ is $A=3.$",
